00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.310
And we have a great
product at GKE.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.750
So I'm having a great time.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.430
MARK MANDEL: All right, cool.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.790
So why don't we start
with an easy question.

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.190
So what is GKE?

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:04.557
What is Google Container Engine?

00:06:04.557 --> 00:06:06.140
CHEN GOLDBERG: Google
Container Engine

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.530
is a platform that
helps you manage

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.890
your containerized applications
the same as Google does.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.590
We in Google have been
managing applications

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:19.670
within containers like Gmail
and Maps for over 12 years now.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.745
And with GKE, we're able
to provide the same service

00:06:22.745 --> 00:06:26.420
and offerings to Google
Cloud Platform users.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.910
This means that when
you are choosing GKE,

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:32.600
you can really focus on
developing your application.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.980
And GKE allows you to quickly
deploy your application

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:39.710
and make it available
to users as a service.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.160
And we are responsible
for all the productization

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:43.520
of your service.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.150
And the differentiation is,
it's an amazing technology

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.630
of Kubernetes, which
is the heart of GKE,

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:54.350
together with Google Cloud
Platform in general, which

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.530
means all the goodness of Google
network and storage and machine

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.350
learning that is available.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:00.750
And on top of
everything, I think

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.270
what's very unique
for Google and we

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.180
are known for is our
operational excellence.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.480
Our site reliability
engineers and the practices

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.390
that we have built
throughout the years

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.430
are being implemented
for users through GKE.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
not that long ago, we

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:16.970
had an amazing episode.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.070
We had the chance to
have John Wilkes talking

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:20.690
about Borg and Kubernetes.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.000
But for those that
have not listened

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.340
to any of our
Kubernetes episodes,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.250
could you tell us a little bit
about like, why is Kubernetes

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:29.450
a thing?

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.610
What is cool about it?

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:33.656
CHEN GOLDBERG: Everything.

00:07:33.656 --> 00:07:34.929
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Of course.

00:07:34.929 --> 00:07:36.720
CHEN GOLDBERG: As I
said before, Kubernetes

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.620
is the heart of Google
Container Engine.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:43.250
It's an open source platform for
automating deployment, scaling,

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:47.900
and operations of containerized
applications across clusters.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.720
With Kubernetes,
you're able, once you

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.870
are familiar with
containers and you

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.820
have microservice
applications, you

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.730
are able to deploy your
applications quickly

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.670
in a predictable way and
scale on the fly, which

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:05.900
means that it's very easy to
meet changing demand together

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:06.890
with Kubernetes.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:07.640
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.460
So then what's the
difference or possibly

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:13.280
the relationship between
Kubernetes and Container

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:13.929
Engine?

00:08:13.929 --> 00:08:14.720
How does that work?

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.095
CHEN GOLDBERG:
The first thing is

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:19.280
that Kubernetes is open
source, and it runs anywhere.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.190
This is also the
main differentiation

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.910
between Borg, our
internal container-centric

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:28.490
infrastructure, and Kubernetes.

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.500
For the team that
founded Kubernetes,

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.049
it was very important
to make it open source,

00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:37.789
because we understand that Cloud
today, while we see that it's

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:41.480
a very popular trend,
still most of the workloads

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.150
are not running in Cloud.

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:44.750
But they are running on-premise.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.840
So we know that if people
want to move to containers

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.300
and they want to be agile
and to quickly deploy,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.210
they are actually looking
to do it not in the cloud

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.460
but in other environments.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.930
So the team really
wanted to make

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.600
something open source that
it would be easily adopted.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:00.620
So that's one.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.760
And the second thing is that
by making it open source,

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:08.420
we are building a very
rich ecosystem around it.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.220
So you have many
other tools that

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.060
are bridging that gap, if its on
developer experience, security,

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:16.341
storage, and network and more.

00:09:16.341 --> 00:09:18.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So then
that means that, basically, I

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.290
can get Kubernetes and
run it basically anywhere.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.860
And every single time I say you
can run Kubernetes everywhere,

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.390
it comes to my mind the
demo that Ray Tsang did,

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.392
where he runs Kubernetes on a
cloud server of Raspberry Pis.

00:09:32.392 --> 00:09:34.850
So it is like one on top of
each other, which is very cool.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.310
So you can run it
virtually anywhere,

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:41.780
but we offer a way to run it on
GKE, Google Container Engine.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.410
What is the benefit of running
it on Google Container Engine

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:46.820
rather than just installing
it on your own cluster?

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.570
CHEN GOLDBERG: First
of all, just spinning

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.840
off a new cluster in Kubernetes
is not something hard to do,

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:52.340
OK?

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:55.760
We actually invested a lot
in making it easy, with tools

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.790
like kubeadm, and
we even emphasized

00:09:57.790 --> 00:09:59.930
that it would be easy
in a consistent way

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:04.140
across different platforms,
wherever you run it.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.410
The value of GKE,
or Container Engine,

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.200
is by the offering
of what happens next.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.730
So you spin off your cluster.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:12.690
This is great.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.910
Then even you start to
deploy your application

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:16.630
on that cluster.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:18.390
How do you keep the
cluster updated?

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:22.310
How do you get the most
recent Kubernetes versions?

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:26.490
Google Container Engine is
where 1.6 is already available.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:30.150
We are always the first one to
make innovation of Kubernetes

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:33.360
available within the
Kubernetes community.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.190
And we are doing it without
compromising quality.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:39.030
So when we make it available
on GKE, we have tested it,

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.790
and we make sure that it will
work well for our customers.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:45.340
We are upgrading
automatically both the master,

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.820
the control plane of the
cluster, and the nodes.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:50.486
And we provide you out of
the box the integration

00:10:50.486 --> 00:10:52.360
to everything that you
need to actually serve

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.310
customers for your application.

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:57.240
So integration with network
and load balancing and storage

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:59.760
and just solving all
the infrastructure

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.560
challenges for you.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:05.220
What I often hear from
developers is two things.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:10.080
GKE is the best and easiest
way to try out Kubernetes,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.474
because it's so easy
to spin off a cluster.

00:11:12.474 --> 00:11:13.890
But the more
important thing, it's

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:17.310
the easiest and fastest way
to make your application

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:18.420
productized.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.430
So if you are a
startup, for example,

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:24.000
and you want to quickly
set up something and maybe

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.430
serve in multiregions, GKE and
GCPs is a very easy way to go.

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
you mentioned updates.

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:35.820
And I'm curious about that
because since Kubernetes

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.980
is built for failure, for
resilience against failure,

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:45.330
I'm assuming that that might
help with update procedures.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.600
But how does it work?

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:51.304
Do I even care when the update
happens from 1.5 to 1.6,

00:11:51.304 --> 00:11:51.845
for instance?

00:11:51.845 --> 00:11:53.345
CHEN GOLDBERG: So
first of all, this

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:56.210
is some of our secret sauce
of how we do it in GKE.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:57.549
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:11:57.549 --> 00:11:59.340
CHEN GOLDBERG: And,
no, you shouldn't care.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:03.150
And of course we make sure that
we will not introduce risks.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:06.390
There is a way, if
someone wants to opt out,

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.280
but within Kubernetes,
we are making sure

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.660
that we are backward compatible.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.100
We can upgrade for you.

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.360
And usually you don't
know and you don't care.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.450
The machines themselves that are
part of the Kubernetes clusters

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.030
are more tricky.

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:24.450
This is area where some would
prefer to opt out of those

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.870
capabilities, especially if
they have in their application

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.990
design some constraints
that we cannot foresee.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.540
But overall we are
making the upgrade

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.070
while we mitigate
the risk, making

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.782
sure your application continues
to serve as you would expect.

00:12:37.782 --> 00:12:38.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's cool.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:39.660
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.201
And you talked about
it a little bit,

00:12:41.201 --> 00:12:43.395
but I'd love to hear
some more detail.

00:12:43.395 --> 00:12:45.999
So, GKE was to integrate
it more with GCP,

00:12:45.999 --> 00:12:48.540
there were certain things out
of the box that were available.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.831
Can you talk a bit about the
sort of features and tools

00:12:50.831 --> 00:12:53.400
that are directly integrated
into the hosted Kubernetes

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.932
solution that we run?

00:12:54.932 --> 00:12:58.200
CHEN GOLDBERG: I think the
most thing to highlight

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.360
is the infrastructure.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.220
For example, RVMs, powered
by a Google Compute Engine.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:06.750
You can have flexible machines.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.030
You can decide exactly
what types of machines

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:13.590
you will have, if they are
the fastest to start up.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.430
This is very unique to GCP.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:19.230
Network, of course,
works out of the box.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:22.140
We allow you to integrate
into the IM capabilities

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:23.820
of the rest of GCP.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.220
And also what we lately
see more and more

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.730
is how GKE customers are
leveraging the machine learning

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:32.820
services available on GCP.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:35.970
So those are just a few
examples of the power of GKE

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.220
together with GCP.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.136
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:38.136 --> 00:13:41.970
So let's talk a
little bit about GKE.

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:48.240
We just had the 1.6 version
of Kubernetes released,

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:51.840
and you mentioned that it
is already available on GKE.

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:54.790
What is the coolest
thing, in your opinion?

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:58.100
What is your favorite feature
of all the things that are new?

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:00.660
CHEN GOLDBERG: I am excited
usually about the big things

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.326
that we are trying
to do, and then there

00:14:02.326 --> 00:14:05.250
are multiple features that
allow us to move forward.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.680
Aparna, in her keynote
at KubeCon in Berlin

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.770
when we announced
1.6, was talking

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.740
about how Kubernetes
is moving forward

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:17.550
in supporting multiworkloads
and multiusers at scale.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.310
And to me that's
the exciting part

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.480
that I look forward this
year with Kubernetes.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:25.080
When you are running
your applications

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.970
at scale within a
cluster, this is where

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:29.424
it becomes the most efficient.

00:14:29.424 --> 00:14:30.590
This is where it's exciting.

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:33.690
This is where you need to
invest less in operating

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:35.490
the cluster itself.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:38.010
So there are two examples
of features that are really

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:39.480
helping us move forward there.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.560
One is supporting 5,000
nodes of a single cluster.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:43.960
This is also available on GCP.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:45.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is huge.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:45.900
MARK MANDEL: That's good.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.270
CHEN GOLDBERG: But it's
not just about right size.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:49.770
It's not all that matters.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.650
We have, for example,
introduced a better

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:56.580
what we call RBAC, Role-Based
Access Control, which gives you

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.300
more [? generality ?] of
permissions within a cluster.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.090
So if before it was harder
to have multiple teams,

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:05.240
for example, work
within a cluster.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:09.900
It meant that you were forced
to have multiple clusters.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.040
Now that's no longer the case.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:13.894
And I think that's
the most exciting.

00:15:13.894 --> 00:15:15.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Basically
the whole idea here

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:18.960
is that, rather than
having-- like at a startup,

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.272
you have maybe three clusters
for three different teams

00:15:22.272 --> 00:15:22.980
working together.

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:25.890
Now you have one cluster that
might be a little bit bigger

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:28.189
but now you have all the
things running together.

00:15:28.189 --> 00:15:28.980
CHEN GOLDBERG: Yes.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:30.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is really cool.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:31.978
CHEN GOLDBERG: Yes,
and just to make sure

00:15:31.978 --> 00:15:33.600
that we don't confuse
anyone, we still

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:37.170
recommend separating development
environment from production

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:37.876
environment.

00:15:37.876 --> 00:15:39.625
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
a very good point.

00:15:39.625 --> 00:15:41.541
CHEN GOLDBERG: But other
than that, definitely

00:15:41.541 --> 00:15:44.070
having multiple teams
or multiple services,

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.205
even in production, serving
from the same cluster.

00:15:46.205 --> 00:15:49.002
MARK MANDEL: So just from
an outside perspective,

00:15:49.002 --> 00:15:50.460
or even from an
inside perspective,

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:54.390
it seems like the community
surrounding Kubernetes is just

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:56.760
growing by leaps and bounds,
even in the last six months

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:57.750
to a year.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.355
Do you have any
involvement in that?

00:15:59.355 --> 00:16:00.540
CHEN GOLDBERG: Yes.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:05.310
I think this was what was most
exciting for me in this role,

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.225
is helping to build
this community.

00:16:07.225 --> 00:16:09.120
Kubernetes as an
open source project

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:13.230
is very unique for the reason
that we encourage and we

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.300
want to have more contribution.

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.360
So we are actively
working on increasing

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:22.590
the percentage of Google
contribution into Kubernetes.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:27.720
When we started, Googlers
contributed 76% of the commits,

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:31.380
and right now it's
in the low 40s.

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:32.160
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:34.860
CHEN GOLDBERG: And it's not
like my team is not growing.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:36.270
We grow fast.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:38.970
We're still hiring, if
anyone is interested.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:41.970
It is just that we make sure
that the community grows

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:43.500
even faster than we do.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:45.510
And that's important for us.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:50.070
We've found that a product
or technology like Kubernetes

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:52.440
will not be
successful without it.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:55.380
Let's take the promise
of "runs anywhere."

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:59.160
For that, we need users to
actually run it anywhere.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:00.300
We know we don't do it.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:03.630
We know that we are
experienced with our GKE users

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.790
and what we know
from Google, but we

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:08.099
have Red Hat, for
example, or CoreOS.

00:17:08.099 --> 00:17:11.520
They are running on-premise
Kubernetes and managing it.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:15.900
Their feedback is very
important for the community.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.140
We see others that are helping
with developer experience

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:22.349
and simplification and
network and security.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:26.010
Just having all of
those expertise together

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:29.160
and getting that
feedback really fast

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:32.135
is what makes Kubernetes
so special in my mind.

00:17:32.135 --> 00:17:33.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I guess we've

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:38.130
been talking around this topic
for basically the whole time.

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:41.310
But we keep on talking
about community,

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:44.970
and I guess that the fact
that it is open source

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.610
helps the community,
but not only.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:50.730
Can you tell us a little
bit about why is Kubernetes

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:52.110
open source to start with?

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:54.910
CHEN GOLDBERG: So, first of
all, as engineering managers

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:57.480
or in general as
engineers, I think

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:01.200
it's very hard to
compete with technologies

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:02.640
that are not open source.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:05.910
I mean, there is a
limit of how many people

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:10.350
a single company can put
into a single product.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:13.260
And I think it's something that
is very clear with Kubernetes.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.130
The amount of investment
right now, it was calculated,

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:20.040
it's about 400
engineering years already.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.300
That's huge.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:24.030
I don't have such a
big team to support it.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:27.330
I mean, for me as an engineering
manager, and also the team,

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:30.720
we think that innovation
pace is unmatchable

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.820
when choosing open source.

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.940
And also for Google
specifically, I

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.715
think open matters,
open source matters,

00:18:37.715 --> 00:18:39.630
but it matters more in Cloud.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.420
We talked about the
portability aspect,

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.470
which is very important.

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:48.790
We keep saying to our customers,
we want you to have a choice.

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:51.390
So you have the freedom to
come to Google Cloud Platform,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.250
but also have the
freedom to leave.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:55.950
We want you to stay because
this is the best cloud for you.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Obviously.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:59.901
MARK MANDEL: We're biased.

00:18:59.901 --> 00:19:03.330
CHEN GOLDBERG: But people can
leave, and they choose to stay.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.010
Openness is not
just about choosing

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:07.490
the platform but
also the flexibility

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:08.970
on which tools to use.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.650
There are so many CI/CD tools.

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:12.390
And that, by the
way, keeps changing.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:15.090
And there will be
always new technologies.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:18.420
So by making it open source,
we know that new technologies

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.390
will continue to be
introduced into Kubernetes

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.556
and supported because it's
open source, because it's

00:19:22.556 --> 00:19:23.520
easy to do it.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.760
And when building a
community in Kubernetes,

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:30.440
we invest probably half of our
time in building Kubernetes

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:32.580
as a technology and
half of the time

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.340
around building the
community and making

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:38.100
it easy to develop
on top of Kubernetes.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:39.570
So if I'm now a
startup that wants

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:42.010
to integrate into
Kubernetes or just

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:44.970
a vendor or even an
enterprise customer,

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:48.100
I think this is a reason
why they like open source.

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.830
You can tailor it
to your own needs.

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:53.310
And we invest a lot
in extensibility.

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:56.640
We care about fragmentation
and in general making sure

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:59.702
that the success of
Kubernetes is sustainable.

00:19:59.702 --> 00:20:00.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:04.030
So you're talking about
the fact that there's

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:08.080
a very big interest in people
building on top of Kubernetes.

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:11.360
And we've actually
interviewed Helm.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.659
We've interviewed a bunch
of different people building

00:20:13.659 --> 00:20:15.700
stuff, like fission also
is on top of Kubernetes,

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.120
a bunch of different projects
that run on Kubernetes,

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:19.990
which is amazing.

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:23.017
So what is the coolest or
the most interesting one

00:20:23.017 --> 00:20:24.100
that you have seen lately?

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:27.640
CHEN GOLDBERG: I wouldn't
say the most interesting,

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:30.940
but I can say something
that I was passionate about.

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:32.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Let's go with that.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:34.231
CHEN GOLDBERG: What I
was investing more in

00:20:34.231 --> 00:20:35.290
is definitely Helm.

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:39.890
Kubernetes started with very
infrastructure-oriented people,

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.410
many ops people.

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:44.290
And last year our
main focus was making

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:47.440
sure it's easy for developers
to start using Kubernetes.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:49.770
And I don't think
we are done yet,

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:53.790
but with Helm, I think it's
amazing the amount of charts

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:56.110
that we have that support
multiple workloads,

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:57.610
different types of workloads.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:00.350
And we make sure that
everything that we produce--

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:03.790
and we say "we" because it's
a community effort, of course,

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:08.440
led by Deis team, to make
sure that everything is tested

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:10.180
and that when we
release new releases,

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.582
we want to make sure that things
are still working as expected.

00:21:13.582 --> 00:21:15.040
So that's one thing
that I'm really

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:17.740
excited about from within
the Kubernetes community.

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:18.970
But that's just one example.

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:20.303
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's awesome.

00:21:20.303 --> 00:21:23.510
Since you're mentioning
Deis, they were on the news

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:28.460
not that long ago, because
they are joining Microsoft.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.370
What is your view
on all of this?

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:31.120
What do you think?

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:32.620
CHEN GOLDBERG: First
of all, I think

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:36.010
this is exciting for
the Kubernetes community

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:39.850
because this is just evidence
to show how companies realize

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:41.500
the value of Kubernetes.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:44.710
And it also validates our belief
that developers want openness

00:21:44.710 --> 00:21:47.590
and flexibility in the cloud.

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:50.680
More specifically
for Deis, Deis are

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:54.190
friends and a very
important member

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:56.650
of the community of Kubernetes.

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:58.520
And I'm really happy for them.

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:01.330
And I hope that with
this acquisition,

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:02.989
they can do more for Kubernetes.

00:22:02.989 --> 00:22:04.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:06.643
Well, we're kind of
running out of time.

00:22:06.643 --> 00:22:08.680
But is there anything
else that you

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:12.130
would like to add, any
topic that we've forgotten,

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:14.590
or anything else that
you'd just like to mention?

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:16.524
CHEN GOLDBERG: Yes.

00:22:16.524 --> 00:22:17.440
We haven't touched it.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.680
We talked about
technology and openness,

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.410
but one thing that I am
passionate about, definitely

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:26.290
as an engineering
manager, is about building

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:27.610
the right culture.

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:32.080
And I think that the right
recipe to build a strong team

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:35.310
is having a very strong
culture and set of values.

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:38.750
That's the thing I like
the most about Kubernetes.

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:42.910
It's about openness and
appreciating feedback,

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:45.490
inclusiveness, and diversity.

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:48.010
From the moment I joined
Kubernetes community,

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:52.280
I felt comfortable
and respected.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:54.130
I hope everybody
else feels like that,

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:56.172
and I would welcome
everybody to join us.

00:22:56.172 --> 00:22:56.880
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:58.305
So to segue from
there, if people

00:22:58.305 --> 00:23:00.430
want to get involved with
the Kubernetes community,

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:01.767
where should they go?

00:23:01.767 --> 00:23:03.350
CHEN GOLDBERG: That's
a good question.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.170
Kubernetes can be
very overwhelming

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.150
with the size of it.

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:10.010
We have a concept which we
call special interest groups.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:12.760
They are smaller groups
within the community

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:16.000
of people working together on an
area they are passionate about.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:19.180
There is about 25
of these, and we

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.040
can add in the notes
exactly linked to that.

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:25.360
I think the best way is just to
choose an area you care about

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:27.940
and join one of these
special interest groups.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:29.650
Then you will know
the people by name,

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:33.070
you can see the faces
on the GVC or Zoom

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:36.945
or whatever meeting tool
you will be using there.

00:23:36.945 --> 00:23:39.290
That would probably
be the easiest one.

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:41.550
I can highlight one specific.

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:44.680
We have the Contrib
Experience special interest

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:47.530
group, which is focusing on
the contributor experience.

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:50.420
So even if you know
nothing about Kubernetes,

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:53.110
but you know something
about developer experience

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:56.410
and open source, that
will be the easiest way

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:57.250
to get involved.

00:23:57.250 --> 00:23:58.499
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's great.

00:23:58.499 --> 00:24:00.670
We will have a bunch of
links from the show notes

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:03.670
to point to of those
special interest groups.

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:06.520
And also we'll have a bunch
of links to different talks

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:08.410
from KubeCon.

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:10.120
I know that your
keynote was really good,

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:11.995
so if people are interested
in learning more,

00:24:11.995 --> 00:24:13.662
I think it's a very
good place to go to.

00:24:13.662 --> 00:24:14.620
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.570
Well, Chen, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:18.370
It was super great to
have you on the podcast.

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:19.828
CHEN GOLDBERG:
Thank you very much.

00:24:19.828 --> 00:24:21.889
MARK MANDEL: Thanks again
to Chen for joining us.

00:24:21.889 --> 00:24:23.680
It was really great to
have a chat with her

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:26.200
and hear about her opinions
and her perspective

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.032
on Container Engine
and Kubernetes.

00:24:28.032 --> 00:24:29.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
and just talking to her,

00:24:29.990 --> 00:24:31.120
because she's awesome.

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:34.810
Now we're going for our Question
of the Week, and as we said,

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:37.090
it is a Question of the
Week coming from Slack,

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:41.770
from the username Shah_HelloAida
or something like that.

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:44.470
So the question is,
if I want to access

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:48.790
private repo from Container
Builder, how do I do it?

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:49.540
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:51.610
So this just depends.

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:52.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.570
I guess before we start
answering the question,

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:57.790
we should mention
what are those things?

00:24:57.790 --> 00:24:59.860
What is Container Builder?

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:03.100
And what is a private repo?

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:04.170
So Container Builder?

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:06.380
MARK MANDEL: Container
Builder is essentially

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:10.926
an automated system for building
containers on Google Platform.

00:25:10.926 --> 00:25:12.800
It can be fired manually,
so you can actually

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:15.980
run it locally to basically use
the cloud to build containers

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:16.830
in the cloud.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:19.130
But you can also have
it run on say, WAN,

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:21.795
and Git repo gets pushed
to, or something like that,

00:25:21.795 --> 00:25:23.420
and then have it
trigger automatically.

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:26.064
It can have multiple build
steps and can do more than just

00:25:26.064 --> 00:25:27.230
building a simple container.

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:29.210
So it's a pretty
nice little piece

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:31.910
of foundation work for
building continuous integration

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:33.290
and continuous delivery systems.

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:34.206
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:34.206 --> 00:25:36.710
What I like about it is the
fact that it allows you to build

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:40.610
containers very easily,
and you can run it directly

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:44.300
from Gcloud Container
Build, submit something.

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:47.200
You don't need to install
Docker on your machine.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:48.800
If you don't have
it, that's fine.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:50.450
You can still build
your containers

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:53.310
and push it to the container
repository, well, Container

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:53.870
Registry.

00:25:53.870 --> 00:25:56.339
Actually, and then
access it from there.

00:25:56.339 --> 00:25:57.380
So that's pretty awesome.

00:25:57.380 --> 00:26:02.120
So to do that, Container Builder
accesses repos and downloads

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:03.080
code from places.

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:06.184
And when it's a public
repo, no matter where it is,

00:26:06.184 --> 00:26:07.850
well, it's public,
so you can access it.

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:09.629
But what if it's private?

00:26:09.629 --> 00:26:11.420
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
you're working probably

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:13.044
on some sort of
commercial application.

00:26:13.044 --> 00:26:15.220
You don't want it to be
exposed the outside world.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:16.595
There's a couple
of options here.

00:26:16.595 --> 00:26:18.770
So Google Cloud
Platform does allow you

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:20.300
to have source repositories.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:22.670
They are private repositories,
private Git repositories

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.260
that come with Google
Cloud Platform.

00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:25.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
they're pretty awesome.

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:26.300
I use them all the time.

00:26:26.300 --> 00:26:27.508
MARK MANDEL: I use them, too.

00:26:27.508 --> 00:26:28.800
They're really super simple.

00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:30.620
So if you're running
Google Cloud Builder

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:32.810
in the same project as
the source repository

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:34.487
that you have your
code in, then there's

00:26:34.487 --> 00:26:35.570
not going to be a problem.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:37.310
It should be able to grab
that code, pull it down,

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:38.430
do what it needs to do.

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:38.930
Sweet.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:40.100
It's perfect.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:41.750
If you have your
source repository

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:44.220
in a different
project, you will need

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.220
to configure some permissions
on that repository

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.590
so that your external
repository can then access it.

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:52.490
So basically give that Builder
project access to that source

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:53.740
repository so it can do that.

00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:54.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Makes sense.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.678
MARK MANDEL: If you have
something like a private Git

00:26:56.678 --> 00:26:58.760
repo on, say, like,
GitHub, then we're

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:00.535
going to need to
get some SSH keys.

00:27:00.535 --> 00:27:02.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
how do you do that?

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:02.630
MARK MANDEL: How do we do that?

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:04.107
So in the future,
hopefully, this

00:27:04.107 --> 00:27:06.440
should be a whole lot easier
and there will be no steps.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:08.660
The solution we talk
about having now

00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:12.620
basically is, store those
keys in a private Google Cloud

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:13.635
Storage bucket.

00:27:13.635 --> 00:27:16.010
So put them somewhere so that
the outside world can't get

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:17.750
to them but that project can.

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:19.910
And then, within
your build step, just

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:22.100
download those keys from
that Git storage bucket,

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:23.770
put them in the
appropriate space,

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:26.536
probably in an SSH
folder, and then you'll

00:27:26.536 --> 00:27:28.910
be able to access your Git
repository pretty much exactly

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:29.940
as you would do normally.

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:30.856
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:27:30.856 --> 00:27:34.767
I wonder if it
could be also doable

00:27:34.767 --> 00:27:37.100
if you started a metadata
server or something like that.

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:40.872
But it's probably easier to
do it on Google Cloud Storage.

00:27:40.872 --> 00:27:43.080
That's actually something
that people do not realize.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:45.770
You can just drag and drop
files from your desktop

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:47.850
to the bucket, which
is pretty amazing.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.100
MARK MANDEL: It's pretty easy.

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:50.390
Then you've got also
a lot of control

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:52.729
because you have all the ACLs,
the Access Control Lists,

00:27:52.729 --> 00:27:54.020
and that kind of stuff as well.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:55.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's a very
good reason to do it that way.

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:58.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so Container
Builder, pretty flexible.

00:27:58.340 --> 00:27:59.180
You can do a lot with it.

00:27:59.180 --> 00:28:00.730
It's actually
really, really cool.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:03.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it
is definitely worth trying.

00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:05.290
I'm going to be
playing with it soon,

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:08.750
because there's this new
thing, the Docker Build, that

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:09.640
is multistep.

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:11.150
I don't know if
you heard about it.

00:28:11.150 --> 00:28:12.290
MARK MANDEL: I have not.

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:14.331
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It allows
you to do things like,

00:28:14.331 --> 00:28:16.100
when you want to build
something and you

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:21.200
want to put all your tooling
and compilers and stuff,

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:23.030
and then you want to
generate a binary.

00:28:23.030 --> 00:28:25.730
And you want that binary to
be in the smallest container

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:27.320
image possible.

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:28.940
So it allows you
to do exactly that.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:30.770
MARK MANDEL: That's similar
to what I do by hand.

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:31.811
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Exactly.

00:28:31.811 --> 00:28:33.240
Less meg files.

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.360
I know, it may sound
a little bit sad

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:37.210
for you to have less meg files.

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:38.715
It's actually a good thing.

00:28:38.715 --> 00:28:40.340
So I'm going to play
with that, and I'm

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:43.070
pretty sure that Container
Builder is a very nice way

00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:43.770
to do that.

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:45.020
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

